  "Leonida is nuked: GitHub repo deleted, Discord gone, creator quits": "",
  "Within an hour on Monday night the biggest GTA VI port lost its Discord server and its GitHub repository, and its creator said he was stepping away.": "",
  "Leonida, the most-starred GTA VI port with more than 33,000 GitHub stars, went offline in under an hour on Monday night. Its Discord server stopped resolving at about 23:07 UTC. By 23:47 its GitHub repository was gone. Around the same time its creator, NotRexed, posted \"I quit guys\" on X, writing that he kept running into problems he couldn't control and was stepping away, and thanking the Discord community.": "",
  "The repository now answers \"not found\", not the \"unavailable\" page GitHub shows after a DMCA takedown, and GitHub's public DMCA log has no notice against it. That points to its owners pulling it rather than Take-Two, though only they can say. Earlier that evening it had moved to a new organization, ProjectLeonida, whose maintainers closed thousands of open pull requests and issues.": "",
  "The code isn't lost. Contributors' forks are still on GitHub, among them the one carrying the combined boot fixes, and a mirror on NotRexed's own Git server holds the code as of 23:30 UTC, though it can no longer sync.": "",
  "We've marked Leonida as nuked on the tracker. It stays on the board for the record but no longer counts in the rankings.": "",
  "NotRexed (@ItsNotRexed)": "",
  "A contributor fork that survives (ASTROWwwW/Leonida)": "",
  "Leonida's Discord server disappears as the project moves to a new GitHub account": "",
  "The 179,000-member server stopped resolving late on Monday. Members say Discord banned it; nobody has said why. Update: the GitHub repository has since been deleted too.": "",
  "Leonida's Discord server went offline late on Monday. At 23:06 UTC its invite still led to a server of about 179,000 members, 74,000 of them online. Within a minute Discord answered that both the invite and the server were unknown. Members say Discord banned it. Neither Discord nor the Leonida team has said why, and from outside we can't tell a ban from a deletion.": "",
  "At first the code was untouched. About an hour earlier the repository had moved from its creator's personal account to a new organization, ProjectLeonida, created at 22:08 UTC. Old links redirected, and issues were still being updated after the server went dark. Around the same time the maintainers closed thousands of open pull requests and issues, cutting the list from nearly 4,900 to about 200.": "",
  "GitHub's public DMCA log shows no notice against the project. The disappearance follows other pressure on GTA VI communities: earlier this month the GTA 6 Mapping Project's Discord vanished without explanation, and in August Take-Two sought subpoenas against Discord to identify the leaker, which Discord said it hadn't been served.": "",
  "Update, 23:47 UTC: the GitHub repository has since been deleted, and its creator says he has quit. See our follow-up.": "",
  "ProjectLeonida/Leonida (moved from ItsNotRexed/Leonida)": "",
  "GTA 6 Mapping Project reportedly targeted by Take-Two, closing Discord that shared leaks": "",
  "Discord denies being served subpoena by Take-Two as GTA 6 leak crackdowns sweep the internet": "",
  "Exclusive: Marvis livestreams GTA VI reaching its menus at about 15 fps": "",
  "He has since published the code as VIctory, but nobody else has reproduced the run yet. Viewers who studied the stream think it is most likely real.": "",
  "A developer who goes by Marvis livestreamed GTA VI running on a PC on Monday, getting as far as the game's menus at roughly 15 frames per second. No public project has shown that much yet: Leonida, XCL and TuxBox all run the game's own code, but their screens are still black or blank.": "",
  "Late on Monday he published the code on GitHub as VIctory, a Rust layer that runs the game's own code natively on Windows. Its README goes further than the stream: boot screens, intro video, start screen and saves work at 30 fps, and loading reaches the open world, where the picture stays black and the graphics card fails within seconds.": "",
  "Viewers who went through the stream closely mostly concluded it is genuine. A staged fake would take a very elaborate setup, and few think that is what happened. The timing also fits: an outside contributor's unmerged change to XCL reports reaching the first menus on the same day.": "",
  "VIctory sits at our Main menu stage, 55%. Its results are still his own, from one PC with an AMD RX 6700 XT; we'll update the entry as others try the code, and take it down if the run turns out to be staged.": "",
  "VIctory: README and status table": "",
  "XCL pull request 195: a retail game shows its first menus (unmerged)": "",
  "Leonida gets GTA VI's executable running, but nothing is on screen yet": "",
  "Contributors' builds run the game's own code for minutes behind a blank window; posts in its Discord say it won't be playable today, but maybe tomorrow.": "",
  "Hours after Cyberleek's executable appeared on Monday, Leonida's contributors began running it through the project's runtime and logging each attempt in a shared issue. On builds that stack about 25 unmerged pull requests, the retail executable now runs its own code for up to three minutes on Windows, Wine and Proton. It sets up the graphics device, gets through audio setup, builds tens of thousands of graphics pipelines and opens a window.": "",
  "That window stays blank. The current wall is a console-only call on the game's command allocators that the PC layer doesn't answer, so frames never finish. None of the boot fixes are in Leonida's main branch yet, and contributors call merging them the bottleneck.": "",
  "Posts in Leonida's Discord server told the same story. Soon after the drop, one said the game won't be playable today, \"but maybe tomorrow\". Later, another said it \"almost boots\", with a black screen and audio that cuts off. We can't check that timeline, and the repo still lists input, saves and streaming as not started.": "",
  "Leonida isn't alone. XCL runs a retail game its notes identify by GTA VI's executable at 30 fps on a black screen, and TuxBox shows the same black window on Proton. All three now sit at our Exe runs stage, 25%. None has drawn a picture.": "",
